  1→/**
  2→ * The HTTP requests inside a shell command the model wrote.
  3→ *
  4→ * A tool call is one string; the requests in it are however many `curl`
  5→ * invocations the shell would run, each with its own URL, headers and body,
  6→ * and each sitting in a structure that says something about whether and when
  7→ * it ran: `;` and a newline are an order, `&&` / `||` / `if` are a condition,
  8→ * `for` / `while` are an unknown count, `&` is no order at all. Reading "the
  9→ * first URL in the text" as "the request" gave batch 4 an attempt that never
 10→ * existed (2026-09-11); this module reads the shell instead.
 11→ *
 12→ * Scope: POSIX-ish shell as the models write it — quotes, backslashes,
 13→ * continuations, `$(…)` and backticks (kept as one unexpanded word), `#`
 14→ * comments, heredoc bodies (skipped), redirections, the control keywords. Not
 15→ * a shell: nothing is expanded or executed; a word that contains `$…` is
 16→ * reported as not literal and its value is not guessed.
 17→ */
 18→
 19→const OPS = new Set([";", ";;", "\n", "&&", "||", "|", "|&", "&", "(", ")"]);
 20→
 21→/** Strip heredoc bodies: `cmd <<'EOF' … EOF` keeps the `<<EOF` word, drops the lines. */
 22→function stripHeredocs(s) {
 23→  const lines = String(s).split("\n");
 24→  const out = [];
 25→  for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
 26→    out.push(lines[i]);
 27→    const m = /<<-?\s*(['"]?)([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\1/.exec(lines[i]);
 28→    if (!m) continue;
 29→    const delim = m[2];
 30→    let j = i + 1;
