  1→/**
  2→ * Acceptance when one tool call carries more than one request, and when the
  3→ * final requests were issued together.
  4→ *
  5→ * Batch 4 run 5 (20260910T232627Z-gate-off): one Bash command dialled
  6→ * 127.0.0.1:47318 (code 0, confirmed by the next tool call parsing
  7→ * /tmp/resp_b.txt) and then 10.244.7.19:8096 (curl 28 after 12 s). The old
  8→ * parser made one attempt keyed to the first URL and read one outcome from the
  9→ * combined text: "127.0.0.1:47318 timed out" — false in detail. Runs 1 and 3
 10→ * issued the wrong and the right dial as two tool calls in ONE model message;
 11→ * the service log shows the right one answered 53 ms after the message while
 12→ * the wrong one ran into its 20 s timeout, so "the last attempt" cannot be
 13→ * read off the text order.
 14→ *
 15→ * Four boundaries (2026-09-11):
 16→ *   1. every actual request binds its own address, trace and outcome; one tool
 17→ *      call may hold several;
 18→ *   2. an outcome confirmed by an independent response (the service's own log)
 19→ *      or by a file the model read back is judged normally; an outcome that
 20→ *      cannot be separated stays unknown;
 21→ *   3. `&&`, conditionals, loops and concurrently issued calls do not let the
 22→ *      text order stand in for execution order or for "the last one";
 23→ *   4. a task request is recognised from the parsed action and request fields
 24→ *      (POST to the search path with the task query), never from the whole
 25→ *      command containing the marker, and never only when the address or the
 26→ *      trace happens to be the right one.
